========================================
PART 4 — GET THE DATA READY (once, when the corpus file arrives)
========================================

Below, FILE is your corpus file, for example:

C:\Users\username\data\common-pile-1gb.jsonl.gz

and the dataset is named common-pile, version v0.1-1gb.

---

11. Look at the File

RUN

python scripts\corpus.py inspect FILE

OUTPUT

"file_format": "jsonl",
"compression": "gzip",
"fields": {
  "text": ["str"],
  "id": ["str"],
  ...
}

Write down:

TEXT_FIELD = the field with the document text (above: text)
ID_FIELD   = the field with the document id (above: id)

---

12. Register It in Supabase

Steps 9 and 10 first.

RUN

python scripts\corpus.py register --name common-pile --version-label v0.1-1gb --file FILE --bucket YOUR_BUCKET_NAME --dataset-type pretraining_corpus

OUTPUT

"status": "registered",

---

13. Upload the Raw File to R2

RUN

python scripts\corpus.py upload-raw --file FILE --name common-pile --version v0.1-1gb

OUTPUT

"object": "ored-ai/datasets/common-pile/v0.1-1gb/common-pile-1gb.jsonl.gz",

---

14. Train the Tokenizer

RUN

python scripts\corpus.py train-tokenizer --input FILE --text-field TEXT_FIELD --vocab-size 16384 --max-bytes 300000000 --name ored-bpe-16k --version v1 --out data\tokenizers\ored-bpe-16k-v1.json

OUTPUT (end)

"vocab_size": 16384,
"sha256": "<64 letters>"

Do this ONCE. Every training run uses this same file.

---

15. Build the Training Shards

RUN

python scripts\corpus.py build-shards --input FILE --text-field TEXT_FIELD --id-field ID_FIELD --tokenizer data\tokenizers\ored-bpe-16k-v1.json --name common-pile --version v0.1-1gb --out data\tokens\common-pile\v0.1-1gb\ored-bpe-16k-v1

OUTPUT (end)

"content_sha256": "<64 letters>",
"counts": { "documents": {...}, "tokens": {...} }

If it stops, run the same command again: it continues where it stopped.

---

16. Upload the Shards to R2

RUN

python scripts\corpus.py publish --dir data\tokens\common-pile\v0.1-1gb\ored-bpe-16k-v1 --name common-pile --version v0.1-1gb

---

17. Mark the Dataset Ready

Use the sha256 printed in step 13 and the document count from step 15:

RUN

python scripts\corpus.py update-registry --name common-pile --version-label v0.1-1gb --set sha256=SHA256_FROM_STEP_13 --set document_count=NUMBER --set status=ready

OUTPUT

"status": "ready",

After this the dataset record can no longer be changed (only deprecated).

---

========================================
PART 5 — TRAIN
========================================

Every train command below uses the same data settings. Write them once:

RUN

set DATA=--set data.tokens.manifest=data\tokens\common-pile\v0.1-1gb\ored-bpe-16k-v1 --set data.tokens.tokenizer=data\tokenizers\ored-bpe-16k-v1.json --set data.tokens.dataset_name=common-pile --set data.tokens.dataset_version=v0.1-1gb

---

18. Measure What Fits on the GPU

RUN

python scripts\pretrain.py --config configs\ored50m.yaml --probe-batch-size

OUTPUT

{"batch_size": 1, "fits": true, "peak_gib": ..., "tokens_per_second": ...}
{"batch_size": 2, "fits": true, ...}
{"batch_size": 4, "fits": true, ...}
{"batch_size": 6, "fits": false, ...}

The default is batch 4 with 8 accumulation steps (= 32 sequences per step).
If a bigger batch fits with room to spare, e.g. 8, use half the accumulation:

--set data.batch_size=8 --set training.grad_accum_steps=4

---

19. Train

RUN

python scripts\pretrain.py --config configs\ored50m.yaml %DATA%

OUTPUT (start)

PRETRAINING RUN: ored50m (ored50m)
GPU: NVIDIA GeForce RTX 5060 Laptop GPU ...
VRAM: ... GiB total, ... GiB free at start
parameters    : 49,894,912
precision     : bf16
batch         : micro-batch 4 x 1024 tokens, grad accumulation 8 -> effective 32 sequences = 32,768 tokens per optimizer step
dataset       : common-pile v0.1-1gb (content ...)

During training:

Epoch   1/5 | train loss ... | val loss ... | best ... | ... tok/s | peak ... GiB  <- new best.pt

---

20. Continue Stopped Training

RUN

python scripts\pretrain.py --config configs\ored50m.yaml %DATA% --resume

It continues after the epoch saved in best.pt.

---

21. Keep best.pt in R2 Too

Step 9 first. Add this to the train command:

--set checkpoint.r2_upload=true

RUN

python scripts\pretrain.py --config configs\ored50m.yaml %DATA% --set checkpoint.r2_upload=true

Every new best.pt replaces ored-ai/checkpoints/ored50m/best.pt in R2.
Only one copy is ever kept.

---

22. If It Runs Out of GPU Memory

The run stops with:

CUDA ran out of memory with micro-batch ...

best.pt is not touched. Then either:

--set data.batch_size=2 --set training.grad_accum_steps=16

or:

--set model.gradient_checkpointing=true

and run step 19 again.

---

========================================
PART 6 — NOT THERE YET
========================================

23. What ored50m Cannot Do Yet

- Train on many PCs.
- Answer questions (scripts\infer.py / scripts\generate.py read the old
  checkpoint format, not ored50m's best.pt).
- Train on the rows in Supabase (ored_training_data).
